```javascript
function checkPrimeNumbers(numbers) {
    // Function to check if a number is prime
    function isPrime(num) {
        // Numbers less than 2 are not prime
        if (num < 2) return false;

        // Check for divisibility from 2 to square root of the number
        for (let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % i === 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    // Iterate through the array and check each number
    numbers.forEach(number => {
        if (isPrime(number)) {
            console.log(number);
        }
    });
}

// Example usage
const numberArray = [4, 7, 10, 13, 16];
checkPrimeNumbers(numberArray);
```

This function does the following:
1. Creates an inner `isPrime()` function to check primality
2. Uses `forEach()` to iterate through the array
3. Checks each number for primality
4. Prints prime numbers to the console
5. Works with an array of 5 numbers
6. Handles numbers less than 2 and efficiently checks primality

In the example, it would print 7 and 13, which are prime numbers.

